let 2: 'Compare the current policy against a geometric schedule and a byte/replay-cost-aware schedule' -- states the schedules to compare but not why cadence is the chosen lever family","Context names the root cause as writing 'the complete reduced ticket state' and retaining 'the prior snapshot and folded sources append-only (I1, *.retired)' -- two non-cadence levers (per-snapshot content; retention/GC) are visible in the very problem statement but neither is acknowledged or explicitly excluded","src/rebar/reducer/_cache.py:14-21 (Invariant I1): retired renames chosen over hard-delete because a delete 'can be resurrected by a delete/add reconciliation (the RC1 rebase class)' -- makes the GC lever unsafe, i.e. a strong positive rationale for choosing cadence instead","docs/adr/0035 Context: replay's positional skip assumes a snapshot's compiled_state fully captures folded events -- a delta-content snapshot would break this, another positive rationale for cadence over the content lever"],"finding":"The plan selects the cadence family (comparing fixed/geometric/byte-cost schedules) but never states a POSITIVE rationale for WHY cadence is the sound lever to research versus the other two visible levers on the amplification: reducing per-snapshot content (e.g. delta/differential snapshots) or reclaiming the permanently-retained *.retired snapshot bytes. Both alternatives are actually ruled out by contracts (Invariant I1 in reducer/_cache.py precludes hard-deleting retired sources as a 'RC1 rebase class' resurrection hazard; delta-content snapshots would break the self-contained-snapshot property that source_event_uuids / rebuild-on-stray / positional-skip replay in ADR 0035 depend on), so a one- or two-sentence rationale would both justify the choice and forestall a reviewer suspecting a content-level fix was overlooked.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fe31b34d04220325a","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Plan section (bullets 1-2) / Context","norm